/-!
# The first cohomology of the twists on the blowup of a regular local surface

Let `(A, 𝔪, κ)` be a regular local ring of dimension two and let `f : X ⟶ S = Spec A` be the
blowing up of `A` in `𝔪`. This file proves part (2) of [Stacks, Tag 0AGS],
$$ H^1(X, \mathcal{O}_X(n)) = 0 \qquad (n \geq -1), $$
and the whole of part (3), `H^1(X, ℱ) = 0` for a quasi-coherent `ℱ` such that `ℱ` or `ℱ(1)` is
globally generated.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_hgroup_one_twistingSheaf_of_two_generators`: the vanishing of part (2) for
  the blowup of `Spec A` in **any** ideal generated by two elements, `A` Noetherian.
* `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_hgroup_one_twistingSheaf_maximalIdeal`: **part (2) of [Stacks, Tag
  0AGS]**, the previous result at the maximal ideal of a two-dimensional regular local ring.
* `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_hgroup_of_two_le_of_two_generators`: `H^p(X, ℱ) = 0` for `p ≥ 2` and
  quasi-coherent `ℱ`, on the blowup in a two-generated ideal. This is part (1) of the tag in the
  affine Rees/`Proj` model; the part (1) proved in
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ResolutionOfSurfaces/Vanishing/RegularLocalBlowup.lean`, is
  stated on `AlgebraicGeometry.blowUp`, a different spelling of the same scheme.
* `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_hgroup_one_of_generatingSections_of_two_generators` and
  `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_hgroup_one_of_generatingSections_maximalIdeal`: **the case of part (3)
  where `ℱ` itself is globally generated**.
* `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_hgroup_one_of_generatingSections_twist_of_two_generators` and
  `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_hgroup_one_of_generatingSections_twist_maximalIdeal`: **the case of
  part (3) where `ℱ(1)` is globally generated**.
* `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_hgroup_one_of_generatingSections_or_twist_maximalIdeal`: **part (3) of
  [Stacks, Tag 0AGS]**, the disjunction of the two.
* `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_rightDerivedPlus_one_of_epi_of_isColimit_cofan`: the dévissage both cases
  run, for a surjection out of a coproduct of copies of one quasi-coherent module.

## How part (3) is proved

The source's clause (3) covers two cases, `ℱ` globally generated and `ℱ(1)` globally generated.
The first is the last paragraph of the proof of [Stacks, Tag 0E7H], already in this development
as `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.TopDegreeBaseChange`
`.isZero_rightDerivedPlus_one_of_generatingSections`: the surjection `⨁_{i ∈ I} 𝒪_X ⟶ ℱ` out of
the free module, together with `H^1(X, 𝒪_X) = 0` and the vanishing of `H^2` on quasi-coherent
modules, kills `H^1(X, ℱ)`.

For the second the source finds a surjection `⨁_{i ∈ I} 𝒪_X(-1) ⟶ ℱ` and repeats that argument
with `H^1(X, 𝒪_X(-1)) = 0`, which is part (2) at `n = -1`. Two things are needed beyond the first
case, and both are available:

* the dévissage asks for the kernel of the surjection to be quasi-coherent, hence for the
  coproduct to be quasi-coherent. Quasi-coherence is closed under arbitrary colimits —
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.QuasicoherentColimits.isQuasicoherent_of_isColimit` — so a coproduct
  of copies of a quasi-coherent module is quasi-coherent, with no finiteness hypothesis on the
  index type. `isZero_rightDerivedPlus_one_of_epi_of_isColimit_cofan` is the first case's
  dévissage with the free module replaced by such a coproduct.
* the surjection itself. Twisting by `𝒪_X(1)` is an autoequivalence `T` of the module sheaves,
  the Rees algebra being generated in degree one
  (`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.TwistFlatness.isEquivalence_twistFunctor_of_adjoin_degree_one`), so
  `T⁻¹` turns the given epimorphism `⨁_{i ∈ I} 𝒪_X ⟶ ℱ(1) = T ℱ` into an epimorphism onto
  `T⁻¹ T ℱ ≅ ℱ` whose source is, `T⁻¹` preserving colimits, a coproduct of copies of
  `T⁻¹𝒪_X ≅ 𝒪_X(-1)`. The last isomorphism is the multiplication
  `𝒪_X(-1) ⊗ 𝒪_X(1) ⟶ 𝒪_X(0)`, an isomorphism by
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjRepresentsF1.isIso_twistMultiplicationMap_of_adjoin`, followed by
  `𝒪_X(0) ≅ 𝒪_X`. Taking `T⁻¹` rather than the twist by `-1` is what makes an addition law for
  the twists — `(ℱ(1))(-1) ≅ ℱ` — unnecessary.

## The route

The source proves part (2) by a descending induction along the filtration of `𝒪_X` by the powers
of the ideal sheaf of the exceptional divisor, whose successive quotients are `i_*𝒪_E(t)` with
`E = 𝐏¹_κ`. That route needs part (1) of [Stacks, Tag 0AGQ] — the identification `E ≅ 𝐏¹_κ` —
which is not available. The route taken here needs only part (2) of that tag, and no induction.

Write `𝔪 = (x₀, x₁)`; the two generators present the Rees algebra as a graded quotient of
`A[T₀, T₁]`, hence `X` as a closed subscheme `r : X ⟶ 𝐏 = 𝐏¹_A`
(`Stacks.Blowup.blowUpSpecToProjSpace`), with `r^*𝒪_𝐏(n) ≅ 𝒪_X(n)`
(`Stacks.Blowup.blowUpSpecToProjSpacePullbackTwistIso`, part (2) of [Stacks, Tag 0AGQ]). Then

* `Stacks.Blowup.isZero_Hgroup_of_isZero_Hgroup_pushforward` ([Stacks, Tag 089W], a closed
  immersion being affine) replaces `H¹(X, 𝒪_X(n))` by `H¹(𝐏, r_*𝒪_X(n))`, and the projection
  formula `Stacks.Blowup.pushforwardUnitTensorIso` ([Stacks, Tag 01E8]) rewrites `r_*𝒪_X(n)` as
  `(r_*𝒪_X)(n)`;
* the ideal-sheaf sequence `0 ⟶ ℐ ⟶ 𝒪_𝐏 ⟶ r_*𝒪_X ⟶ 0` of the closed immersion stays short exact
  after twisting by the invertible `𝒪_𝐏(n)`
  (`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.TwistFlatness.shortExact_map_twistFunctor_of_adjoin_degree_one`),
  so `Stacks.Coherent.isZero_Hgroup_X₃_of_shortExact` reduces the vanishing to
  `H¹(𝐏, 𝒪_𝐏(n)) = 0` and `H²(𝐏, ℐ(n)) = 0`;
* the first is Serre's computation on the projective line,
  `Stacks.ProjectiveSpace.Cohomology.isZero_lineCohomology_one_of_neg_one_le` ([Stacks, Tag
  01XT]), and this is the only place the hypothesis `n ≥ -1` is used;
* the second is the cohomological dimension of `𝐏¹_A`: it is separated and covered by the two
  affine opens `D₊(T₀)`, `D₊(T₁)`, so [Stacks, Tag 01XI] kills every quasi-coherent sheaf in
  degrees `≥ 2`. The ideal sheaf `ℐ` is the kernel of `𝒪_𝐏 ⟶ r_*𝒪_X`, which is coherent
  (`Stacks.Blowup.isCoherent_kernel_projSpaceQuotientMap`) hence quasi-coherent, and so is its
  twist.

## Implementation notes

The blowup is the affine model `Stacks.Blowup.blowUpSpec 𝔪 = Proj (⨁ₙ 𝔪ⁿ)`, which is the model
the twists `𝒪_X(n) = Stacks.Blowup.blowUpTwist` are defined on and the one part (2) of [Stacks,
Tag 0AGQ] is stated in; the base `Spec A` being affine, this `Proj` is the whole blowup.

Nothing of the regular local ring is used except that its maximal ideal has two generators, so
`isZero_hgroup_one_twistingSheaf_of_two_generators` is stated for an arbitrary ideal with two
generators over a Noetherian ring. That is a generalization of the tag, not a weakening.

The monoidal structure on the modules over `Proj 𝒜` has to be present at two spellings of the
ring sheaf — `X.ringCatSheaf` and the `sheafCompose` spelling `twistingSheaf` states its own
type in — because instance search does not cross between them; both instances already exist in
the library and are re-enabled here by `attribute [local instance]`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AGS](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AGS)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AGQ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AGQ)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01XT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01XT)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01XI](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01XI)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 089W](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/089W)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01E8](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01E8)
-/

